---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Leaderboard from "../components/Leaderboard.astro";
import Num from "../components/Num.astro";
import { leaderboard, ranked, belowLine, POPULARITY, byName, n, generation, href }
  from "../lib/data";

const best = ranked[0];
const worst = ranked[ranked.length - 1];
const families = [...new Set(leaderboard.models.map((m) => m.family))];
---

<Base
  wide
  title="Sixteen models, one line"
  chapter="Two — the models"
  standfirst="Every model here was trained on the same corpus, evaluated on the same held-out recipes, and scored against the same frequency table. The baseline is drawn where it falls."
>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      The column that matters is <b>served</b>: the score a model would
      actually achieve if you deployed it. For most models that is the score
      of its exported vectors, because vectors are all they produce. For two
      of them it is the score of their own conditional rule, which is better
      — that discrepancy is <a href={href("/native/")}>the next section</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      {belowLine.length} of {leaderboard.n_models} models finish below{" "}
      <Num value={POPULARITY} tone="accent" />. The worst,{" "}
      <code>{worst.model}</code>, reaches{" "}
      <Num value={worst.best_recall_at_10} tone="below" /> — it is not
      competing with the baseline so much as failing to do the task at all.
    </p>
  </div>

  <Leaderboard />

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>What is not being claimed</h2>
    <p>
      Not that embeddings are useless. <code>{best.model}</code> reaches{" "}
      <Num value={best.best_recall_at_10} tone="above" />, comfortably above
      the line, and several others clear it honestly.
    </p>
    <p>
      The claim is narrower and more awkward: <b>if you had picked a method
      from this list without measuring, you would more likely than not have
      shipped something worse than counting.</b> All {families.length}{" "}
      families are represented — matrix factorisation, graph convolution,
      word2vec objectives, autoencoders, a set transformer — and being below
      the line is not confined to any one of them.
    </p>
    <h3>Why the baseline is not a strawman</h3>
    <p>
      A popularity recommender is the thing every recommender paper includes
      to be beaten. It has no parameters, takes a single pass over the corpus
      to build, and returns the same ten items to every user. On this task,
      over {n(generation.recipes)} recipes and {n(generation.vocab)}{" "}
      ingredients, it is a genuinely hard target — because recipes are
      dominated by a small number of ingredients, and a model has to earn its
      way past that before it has learned anything at all.
    </p>
    <p class="next"><a href={href("/native/")}>Continue: the vector is the lossy part →</a></p>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .next { margin-top: var(--s-6); font-size: var(--t-md); }
  code { background: var(--paper-sunk); padding: 0 0.25em; }
</style>
